            "Cubic system. Crystals form rhombic dodecahedra or trapezohedra, often rounded by transport. No cleavage; conchoidal fracture. Hardness 7 to 7.5; specific gravity 3.74 to 3.94; bright vitreous lustre; refractive index 1.750 to 1.760. Singly refractive, no pleochroism; inert under UV. The spectroscope shows the characteristic almandine bands near 505, 527 and 576 nm. Fine rutile needles and small apatite or zircon crystals are classic inclusions. It is not treated.",
            "The name was given in 1898, after the colour of the rhododendron, to the garnets of Cowee Valley, Macon County (North Carolina, United States). Tanzania: the Umba valley, the Lindi area and Tunduru, now the main source; Umba is also the source of “Malaya” garnet. Kenya, Mozambique, Madagascar, Sri Lanka, India (Odisha), Zambia and Brazil complete the production.",
            "A stone of inspiration, sensuality and confidence, said to warm the heart and encourage creativity. It is associated with the heart and root chakras, the sign of Capricorn and, like all garnets, the month of January. Cleansing under running water; recharging in gentle sunlight."
